21:44:43 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: It’s understandable to react strongly to unfamiliar customs, especially when they involve family roles, hospitality, religion, mourning, or public etiquette. But “Arab customs” covers a very large and diverse set of societies—across more than 20 countries, many religions, urban and rural settings, and different generations—so it’s hard to make a fair blanket comparison. Some practices may feel more formal or emotionally expressive than what you’re used to: elaborate hospitality, strong expectations around family obligations, modesty norms, or communal celebrations and mourning. But every culture has practices that can seem “intense” from the outside, including strict social etiquette, individualism, drinking culture, dating norms, military traditions, or religious observances. A more useful question is: *Which specific custom are you thinking of, and in which place or community?* Then it can be understood in context rather than as a trait of Arabs generally.
